    # reserveAndEmitDLEntry guard (s109): source-read (NEXT.md, s97/s98) found
    # arg_r4 (saved to ctx->r16/$s0 at entry) is dereferenced as a write
    # target almost immediately (MEM_W(0X0, ctx->r16) = 0xB5000000; then
    # MEM_W(0X4, ctx->r16) = 0;) -- same unguarded-pointer-arg signature as
    # the rest of the hangar-tick family (9th/10th/11th classes). The
    # function's real epilogue (L_8000CE7C-equivalent, i.e. the $ra/$s1/$s0
    # restore + $sp+=0x20 sequence at guest 0x8000CE7C-0x8000CE90) is NOT
    # reachable via a clean existing label -- it falls out of L_8000CE58,
    # which is itself reached by a different code path's cleanup writes, so
    # jumping there from a guard placed before those registers hold sane
    # values would execute unrelated work. A dedicated label
    # (L_dlentry_guard_exit) is inserted immediately before the restore
    # sequence instead of reusing L_8000CE58, so the guard's goto only ever
    # runs the restore + return, never that cleanup code.
    #
    # Placement: NOT immediately after the ctx->r16 assignment (0x8000CB98)
    # as NEXT.md's literal wording suggested -- at that point $ra/$s1 are not
    # yet saved to their stack slots (0x18/0x14 off $sp), only $s0's OLD
    # value is (0x8000CB94, before reassignment). Jumping to a $ra/$s1/$s0
    # restore-from-stack epilogue before those slots are written would load
    # uninitialized stack bytes into $ra and corrupt the return address.
    # The earliest point where all three slots are populated is right after
    # the function's own existing sltu/bne bounds check, which saves $ra to
    # 0x18($sp) on both the taken and fall-through paths (0x8000CBAC) -- so
    # the guard is anchored on the fall-through copy of that save (uniquely
    # identified by the immediately-following 0x8000CBB0 lui, which only the
    # fall-through copy precedes; the taken-branch copy is followed by
    # `goto L_8000CC90;` instead). This is still strictly before the first
    # dereference of ctx->r16 (0x8000CBBC). U32() truncation and the
    # 0x80000000-0x80800000 RDRAM bound follow s99-s101's convention exactly
    # (ctx->r16 is `gpr`/uint64_t).
