    # updateHangarBayScene (s110, 17th crash class, first seen s100): confirmed
    # real name via symbol_files/main_overlay.txt ("hangar bay camera/scene
    # update, gated hangarCameraState 0x800CC3E4==2, indexes per-bay tables by
    # gHangarCurrentBay 0x800CD6E8"). Source-read the whole function
    # (funcs_40.c:7117-13921, ~6800 lines -- by far the largest function
    # guarded in this family so far) before writing anything, per every prior
    # session's convention.
    #
    # This function does NOT have a single "the" bad pointer the way
    # composeNpcOrientationMatrix/activateAnimationKeyframe/
    # projectModelBoundsToScreen did -- it repeats the *same* unguarded
    # "table[byte_index] -> load a pointer out of the entry -> dereference
    # that pointer directly" shape five separate times, each keyed off a
    # different one of four adjacent index bytes at 0x800D0000-relative
    # offsets -0x2918 (gHangarCurrentBay itself), -0x291A, -0x291B, -0x291C
    # (per-spline waypoint/segment indices, read fresh via `lbu` at each use
    # site rather than cached once): the byte scales a fixed table (stride 36
    # or 632 bytes, the classic shift/add/sub multiply-by-constant idiom) into
    # a per-waypoint keyframe-table entry address, and offset +0xC of THAT
    # entry is loaded as a raw pointer and immediately dereferenced at +0x0/
    # +0x4/+0x8 (a vec3) with no null/range check -- textbook match for the
    # rest of the family's signature, just repeated instead of singular.
    # Three more entry+0xC/+0x10/+0x14 reads elsewhere in the function (guest
    # 0x800ACDD0, 0x800AD004, and a third near 0x800AD3xx not transcribed
    # here) copy struct fields directly out of the SAME kind of table entry
    # without a second indirection -- lower risk (one fewer pointer hop) and
    # not guarded this session; if a future soak's backtrace lands on one of
    # those instead, they're the same fix, just not the highest-probability
    # site given the double-indirection candidates below crash on ANY bad
    # entry pointer, not just a bad entry address.
    #
    # Guarded all five double-indirection sites found by this source read
    # (all inline in updateHangarBayScene's own body, matching the crash
    # backtrace's "fault resolves directly inside updateHangarBayScene, no
    # callee frame" description) rather than picking one, because each keys
    # off a different index byte / table -- guarding only the first found
    # would leave the other four able to crash on the very next soak. Same
    # env var and log tag across all five (distinguished by a `site` letter),
    # same U32()-truncated 0x80000000-0x80800000 RDRAM bound as every prior
    # guard in this family (the loaded value is `gpr`/uint64_t; a real guest
    # pointer sign-extends on assignment, s99's lesson).
    #
    # Epilogue: no existing label sits at the function's real restore
    # sequence (falls out of `after_103:` unlabeled), so a dedicated
    # `L_hangarbay_guard_exit` label is inserted immediately before it (same
    # technique as s109's `L_dlentry_guard_exit`) -- ABI-safe from all five
    # guard sites since every register that sequence restores ($ra, $s0-$s7,
    # $f20, $f22) is already saved to its prologue stack slot by
